1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,333
Mandate Wallet gives an AI agent a limited
payment authority. In this

2
00:00:05,333 --> 00:00:11,556
example, we will connect it to Claude
Desktop and ask Claude to obtain an

3
00:00:11,556 --> 00:00:17,333
ETH statistics sample and write a short
brief. The owner sets the budget;

4
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:20,000
the agent performs only approved
purchases.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,714
We start with Claude Desktop signed in,
Node twenty-four and Anvil

6
00:00:24,714 --> 00:00:29,429
installed, and the Mandate source checkout
prepared. This is the current

7
00:00:29,429 --> 00:00:33,714
source-based setup, not a standalone
installer. The demonstration uses a

8
00:00:33,714 --> 00:00:38,000
local test chain, mock USDC and a
controlled sample API.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,846
Open the setup wizard and choose Local.
Mandate starts an isolated chain,

10
00:00:45,846 --> 00:00:54,346
deploys the smart account and prepares one
mock USDC. The task budget is

11
00:00:54,346 --> 00:01:03,500
zero point one five USDC, the maximum
payment is zero point one, and the

12
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:10,692
session lasts one hour. These are
permission limits, separate from the

13
00:01:10,692 --> 00:01:12,000
wallet balance.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,214
Choose Claude Desktop and download this
account’s Guided connection package.

15
00:01:20,214 --> 00:01:28,429
In Claude, open Settings, Extensions and
Advanced settings, then select

16
00:01:28,429 --> 00:01:37,464
Install extension. Review the package and
enable it. The Guided package

17
00:01:37,464 --> 00:01:46,500
already points to this local
configuration, so no Agent profile or

18
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:56,357
Connection file needs to be selected. The
older manual extension remains a

19
00:01:56,357 --> 00:01:58,000
separate option.

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,667
Start a new conversation and ask Claude to
read the wallet context without

21
00:02:06,667 --> 00:02:14,000
paying. Approve the tool request for this
demonstration. Check the network,

22
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,667
account, session, budget and balance. The
connection makes tools available;

23
00:02:20,667 --> 00:02:26,000
it does not increase the approved payment
authority.

24
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,429
Next, ask Claude to find the configured
ETH sample service and quote it

25
00:02:33,429 --> 00:02:40,286
without purchasing. The service costs zero
point zero one mock USDC. The

26
00:02:40,286 --> 00:02:46,000
wallet checks the approved recipient and
request scope. A service-directory

27
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
listing alone cannot authorize a new
payment.

28
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:58,161
Now authorize Claude to purchase this
approved sample. The payment settles,

29
00:02:58,161 --> 00:03:06,323
but the controlled service deliberately
fails its first response. Ask Claude

30
00:03:06,323 --> 00:03:15,226
to retry the same step. Mandate reuses the
original order and settlement

31
00:03:15,226 --> 00:03:24,871
instead of paying again. The service then
returns the sample, and the result

32
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:34,516
checker validates it. The balance is zero
point nine nine USDC, with one

33
00:03:34,516 --> 00:03:36,000
payment recorded.

34
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:44,217
Claude can now write a brief using the
validated sample and identify it as

35
00:03:44,217 --> 00:03:50,087
demonstration data. In the workspace,
inspect payment, delivery and result

36
00:03:50,087 --> 00:03:55,957
validation separately. The receipt
provides the settlement hash and order

37
00:03:55,957 --> 00:04:01,826
identity. A successful token transfer
alone does not establish successful

38
00:04:01,826 --> 00:04:03,000
service delivery.

39
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,580
End the local practice session to revoke
its authority, then ask Claude to

40
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:18,840
purchase the previously unused BTC sample.
The request is rejected before

41
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:26,100
another payment. The earlier ETH receipt
remains intact. This local tutorial

42
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:32,040
uses its test initializer for revocation;
public-testnet permission changes

43
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:36,000
require the owner’s external wallet
confirmation.

44
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,667
The complete flow is setup, connect,
inspect, purchase, recover and revoke.

45
00:04:40,667 --> 00:04:44,485
These screens demonstrate a local test
system. Public-testnet transaction

46
00:04:44,485 --> 00:04:49,152
evidence is provided separately, and this
recording does not represent a

47
00:04:49,152 --> 00:04:50,000
mainnet purchase.
